Q: How to prevent TIdHTTPServer sending HTML content when KeepAlive = true? A: <p><strong>UPDATE:</strong></p>
<p>The root cause of your problem is that you are using a <em>very</em> outdated version of Indy (10.6.0.5122).</p>
<p>RAD Studio XE6 was released in April 2014. At that time, the <code>TIdResponseInfo::WriteContent()</code> method always sent a body text unconditionally, which in your case is the default HTML when the <code>AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText</code> property is blank.  By default, if you do not call <code>WriteContent()</code> yourself, <code>TIdHTTPServer</code> calls it for you, but only if <code>AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText</code> or <code>AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentStream</code> has data to send.</p>
<p>By calling <code>WriteContent()</code> yourself, you are forcing body text to be sent to your client, even if no body text should be sent (ie, in a <code>204</code> response).</p>
<p>The behavior of <code>TIdHTTPResponseInfo::WriteContent()</code> was changed in November 2014 (after RAD Studio XE7 was released) so that it would no longer send any body text for a <code>HEAD</code> request or a <code>1xx</code>, <code>204</code>, or <code>304</code> response, per the HTTP RFCs.</p>
<p>To fix your issue, you can simply not call <code>WriteContent()</code> when you don't have any text to send back to your client, eg:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>void __fastcall TMainForm::HTTPCommandGet (TIdContext *AContext, TIdHTTPRequestInfo *ARequestInfo, TIdHTTPResponseInfo *AResponseInfo)
{
    TCommandHandler Handler;

    // Initialize.
    Handler.Context                 = AContext;
    Handler.RequestInfo             = ARequestInfo;
    Handler.ResponseInfo            = AResponseInfo;
    Handler.ListHistory             = ListHistory;
    Handler.EditVehiclesURI         = EditVehiclesURI;
    Handler.ListVehiclesResponse    = ListVehiclesResponse;
    Handler.ListVehiclesAction      = ListVehiclesAction;
    Handler.ListVehiclesCondition   = ListVehiclesCondition;
    Handler.BtnVehiclesLimitsAsText = BtnVehiclesLimitsAsText;
    Handler.ListVehicles            = ListVehicles;
    Handler.EditEquipmentURI        = EditEquipmentURI;
    Handler.ListEquipmentResponse   = ListEquipmentResponse;
    Handler.TreeEquipment           = TreeEquipment;
    Handler.ListEquipment           = ListEquipment;
    Handler.EditStatusURI           = EditStatusURI;
    Handler.ListStatusResponse      = ListStatusResponse;
    Handler.ListStatus              = ListStatus;
    Handler.EditResultsURI          = EditResultsURI;
    Handler.ListResultsResponse     = ListResultsResponse;
    Handler.ListResults             = ListResults;

    // Add request to 'History' list-view.
    TThread::Synchronize(NULL, &amp;Handler.LogRequest);

    // Process request.
    TThread::Synchronize(NULL, &amp;Handler.ProcessRequest);

    // Check if request should be ignored.
    if (AResponseInfo-&gt;ResponseNo &lt;= 0)
    {
        // Prevent sending response.
        AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText = _D(&quot;&quot;);
        AResponseInfo-&gt;HeaderHasBeenWritten = true;
        return;
    }

    // Generate headers.
    AResponseInfo-&gt;WriteHeader();

    // If only headers requested, clear content.
    if (ARequestInfo-&gt;CommandType == hcHEAD)
        AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText = _D(&quot;&quot;);

    // Send response.
    String S;
    if (!(
        (ARequestInfo-&gt;CommandType == hcHEAD) ||
        ((AResponseInfo-&gt;ResponseNo / 100) == 1) ||
        (AResponseInfo-&gt;ResponseNo == 204) ||
        (AResponseInfo-&gt;ResponseNo == 304)
    ))
    {
        // NOTE: WriteContent() clears ContentText and ContentStream properties.
        S = AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText;
        AResponseInfo-&gt;WriteContent();
    }

    // Add response to 'History' list-view.
    AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText = S;
    try
    {
        TThread::Synchronize(NULL, &amp;Handler.LogResponse);
    }
    __finally
    {
        // Clear content.
        AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText = _D(&quot;&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>A better option would be to upgrade to a newer version of C++Builder (the current version is 13.1) or at least a newer version of Indy (the current version is 10.6.3.14).</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>(Original Answer)</strong>:</p>
<p>What you are experiencing sounds like normal and expected behavior on the server side. The client sends a request, it gets a response. Period. Regardless of whether <code>KeepAlive=true</code> is used or not. A <code>POST</code> request requires a response, just like any other request.  If you don't send a response yourself, <code>TIdHTTPServer</code> will send one for you after the <code>OnCommandGet</code> handler has exited.</p>
<p>You are not seeing the server send a left-over response from a previous request. You are seeing a response to the current response. <code>TIdHTTPServer</code> doesn't carry response data between requests, as each triggering of the <code>OnCommandGet</code> event will receive a fresh set of <code>ARequestInfo</code>/<code>AResponseInfo</code> objects.</p>
<p>Besides, <code>TIdHTTPServer</code> doesn't send any default HTML on a <code>204</code> response anyway (or at all for a <code>HEAD</code> response). So, there should be no possible way that your client is receiving the HTML that you have shown. Even if <code>WriteContent()</code> were being called multiple times, which it would not be in this case, since you are clearing the <code>ContentText</code> after calling <code>WriteContent()</code> the 1st time, so <code>TIdHTTPServer</code> will skip calling it internally a 2nd time.</p>
<p>Also, there is no possible way that <code>TIdHTTPServer</code> could be sending <code>POST /equipment</code> in a response, so the fact that you are seeing that text in your <code>EIdHTTPProtocolException</code> log message suggests you are manually adding that text in your log, yes?</p>
<p>It sounds more like your client is probably just not reading each response fully, and is leaving behind response data that is then read the next time it sends a request. That would be a client-side issue, not a server-side issue. This is easy to verify by simply having the client log the actual HTTP messaging back and forth, which you can do by attaching one of the <code>TIdLog...</code> components to <code>TIdHTTP</code>.</p>
<p>In any case, the only real problem I see with your server code is your <code>OnCommandGet</code> handler is calling <code>WriteHeader()</code> directly when it doesn't need to. Let <code>TIdHTTPServer</code> manage that for you. At the very least, your clearing of the <code>ContentText</code> should be in a <code>try..finally</code> block in case an exception is thrown by <code>TThread::Synchronize()</code> (thus skipping your clearing of the <code>ContentText</code>), eg:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>void __fastcall TMainForm::HTTPCommandGet (TIdContext *AContext, TIdHTTPRequestInfo *ARequestInfo, TIdHTTPResponseInfo *AResponseInfo)
{
    TCommandHandler Handler;

    // Initialize.
    Handler.Context              = AContext;
    Handler.RequestInfo          = ARequestInfo;
    Handler.ResponseInfo            = AResponseInfo;
    Handler.ListHistory          = ListHistory;
    Handler.EditVehiclesURI      = EditVehiclesURI;
    Handler.ListVehiclesResponse    = ListVehiclesResponse;
    Handler.ListVehiclesAction    = ListVehiclesAction;
    Handler.ListVehiclesCondition   = ListVehiclesCondition;
    Handler.BtnVehiclesLimitsAsText = BtnVehiclesLimitsAsText;
    Handler.ListVehicles            = ListVehicles;
    Handler.EditEquipmentURI        = EditEquipmentURI;
    Handler.ListEquipmentResponse   = ListEquipmentResponse;
    Handler.TreeEquipment          = TreeEquipment;
    Handler.ListEquipment          = ListEquipment;
    Handler.EditStatusURI          = EditStatusURI;
    Handler.ListStatusResponse    = ListStatusResponse;
    Handler.ListStatus            = ListStatus;
    Handler.EditResultsURI        = EditResultsURI;
    Handler.ListResultsResponse  = ListResultsResponse;
    Handler.ListResults          = ListResults;

    // Add request to 'History' list-view.
    TThread::Synchronize(NULL, &amp;Handler.LogRequest);

    // Process request.
    TThread::Synchronize(NULL, &amp;Handler.ProcessRequest);

    // Check if request should be ignored.
    if (AResponseInfo-&gt;ResponseNo &lt;= 0)
    {
        // Prevent sending response.
        AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText = _D(&quot;&quot;);
        AResponseInfo-&gt;HeaderHasBeenWritten = true;
        return;
    }

    // Send response.
    String S = AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText;    // NOTE: WriteContent() clears ContentText and ContentStream properties.
    AResponseInfo-&gt;WriteContent();

    // Add response to 'History' list-view.
    AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText = S;
    try
    {
        TThread::Synchronize(NULL, &amp;Handler.LogResponse);
    }
    __finally
    {
        // Clear content.
        AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText = _D(&quot;&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>That being said, you might want to consider changing your <code>TCommandHandler</code> to not manipulate the <code>AResponseInfo</code> directly, but rather to save the required values in the <code>TCommandHandler</code> itself, and then assign the values to the <code>AResponseInfo</code> after <code>Handler.ProcessRequest()</code> has returned.  In fact, it's probably not a good idea to sync the entire <code>Handler.ProcessRequest()</code> call, only the UI portions of it. Do as much work as you can in the <code>OnCommandHandler</code> thread rather than in the main UI thread.</p>
<p>This would also solve the issue of you needing to restore the <code>AResponseInfo-&gt;ContentText</code> just to call <code>Handler.LogResponse()</code>.</p>

Q: Disable short multi-line string concatenation in Ruff A: <p>I did not find linter settings two years later. There is one in Black, but Ruff equivalent seems to be missing.</p>
<p>As a quick workaround you can drop a comment between strings:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>raise RuntimeError(
    f&quot;Multicall retry failed\n&quot;
    # Ruff hack: wants to concatenate multilines strings no matter what you do
    f&quot;Encountered a contract that cannot be called even after dropping multicall batch size to 1 and switching providers, bailing out.\n&quot;
    f&quot;Fallback attempts {i}, max attempts {fallback_attempts}.\n&quot;
    f&quot;Manually figure out how to work around / change RPC providers.\n&quot;
    f&quot;Original provider: {provider} ({provider_name}), fallback provider: {fallback_provider} ({active_provider_name}), chain {chain_id}, block {block_identifier_str}, batch size: 1.\n&quot;
    f&quot;Exception: {e}.\n&quot;
) from e
</code></pre>
<p>If there is ever a linter setting for this, please bump on this.</p>

